    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Twelve walled cities have been excavated, five of them large: Beikthano, Maingmaw, Binnaka, Halin and Sri Ksetra, the last more than 13 km round and larger than Pagan or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandalay">Mandalay</a>; three have been World Heritage Sites since 2014.</li><li>The Pyu were the first in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a> to adapt Brahmic script to a tonal language, and their calendar, begun at Sri Ksetra on 22 March 638, became the Burmese calendar still used in Myanmar.</li><li>Nanzhao's raids from 754 culminated in 832, when its warriors overran the Pyu country and carried off 3,000 prisoners from Halin; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamar-people">Burmans</a> then took over the Pyu realm, and by the 13th century the Pyu had become Burmans.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Tang records describe the Pyu as a humane people to whom war was virtually unknown, who wore <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> rather than silk so as not to kill silkworms, and whose boys entered the monastery from the age of seven until twenty.</p></aside>
    </section>

