    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/iron-working"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/iron-working.jpg" alt="Iron working" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/iron-working">Iron working</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bas_fourneau.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>By 1500 BCE people in the dry zone near the Irrawaddy worked bronze, grew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> and kept chickens and pigs, and by 500 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-working">iron-working</a> settlements in the Samon valley south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandalay">Mandalay</a> traded with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. In about the 2nd century BCE the Tibeto-Burman-speaking Pyu entered the Irrawaddy valley from present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a> by the Taping and Shweli rivers and founded settlements across the plains around the confluence of the Irrawaddy and Chindwin. Their realm was longer than it was wide, from Sri Ksetra in the south to Halin in the north, and the Tang records count 18 Pyu states, nine of them walled cities, covering 298 districts.</p><p>The cities rose in the three great irrigated regions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-burma">Upper Burma</a>: the Mu valley, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyaukse">Kyaukse</a> plains and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minbu">Minbu</a> region. Beikthano, named after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a> and situated near Taungdwingyi, is the oldest urban site yet excavated, its remains dated 200 BCE to 100 CE, a rectangular fortress of 3 by 1 kilometres enclosing about 300 hectares behind walls six metres thick, perhaps the first capital of a culturally and even politically uniform state in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a>. Halin's wooden gates are dated to 70 CE, and the city grew to 664 hectares, twice Beikthano's size; Maingmaw and Binnaka on the Kyaukse plains, with their identical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> coins and funerary urns, may be its contemporaries. The Burmese chronicles make Tagaung, a small Pyu site north of Mandalay, the home of the first Burmese kingdom.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/anawrahta"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anawrahta.jpg" alt="Anawrahta" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/anawrahta">Anawrahta</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anawrahta_at_National_museum.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Phyo WP, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Pyu economy rested on agriculture and trade. Every important settlement lay in one of the three irrigated regions, where the Pyu grew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, perhaps of the japonica variety, and built the dams, canals and weirs whose techniques passed to Pagan; King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anawrahta">Anawrahta</a> would turn the same three regions into the rice granaries of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-burma">Upper Burma</a> in the 1050s. The population of the realm was probably a few hundred thousand, and the size of the cities, 660 to 1,400 hectares against Pagan's 140, suggests that most people lived within the walls.</p><p>Two trade routes crossed the realm. An overland road between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> ran across northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> from at least 128 BCE, and Roman embassies to China passed along it in 97 and 120 CE; but most trade went by sea through the southern states, which then lay near the coast because the Irrawaddy delta had not yet formed, so that ships from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> could sail up to Prome. Goods from 2nd-century northwest India, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> have been found at Beikthano, and Pyu objects along the Arakan coast, on the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanintharyi-region">Tenasserim</a> coast and as far east as Óc Eo. The Pyu minted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> coins from the 5th century, the earliest showing a conch and a srivatsa and often pierced to serve as amulets; they were the model for most first-millennium coinage of mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>, and after they ceased in the late 9th century no coins were struck in Burma until the 19th.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Pyu built for defence. Beikthano's walls were six metres thick; Halin, rectangular with curved corners, had brick walls 3.2 by 1.6 km, four main gates at the cardinal points and twelve in all, and a moat on every side but the south, where dammed land made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reservoir">reservoirs</a>. Sri Ksetra's circular brick walls stood 4.5 metres high with twelve gates guarded by huge devas, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> fields filling its northern half meant that with its moats and walls the city could withstand a long siege. Yet the Tang records describe the Pyu as a humane and peaceful people to whom war was virtually unknown. The chronicles tell of alliances between states, such as Beikthano with Sri Ksetra.</p><p>The end came from the north. Burman "swift horsemen" of the Nanzhao Kingdom entered the upper Irrawaddy valley in raids that the Tang chronicles date from 754 or 760; by 763 the Nanzhao king Ko-lo-feng had conquered the upper valley, and the raids intensified in 800–802 and 808–809. In 832, by the Chinese account, Nanzhao warriors overran the Pyu country and carried off 3,000 prisoners from Halin, and in 835 they raided Mi-ch'en. The Pyu did not vanish with those 3,000: no site shows a violent overthrow, and radiocarbon dating puts human activity at Halin until about 870. The raids weakened the states and let the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamar-people">Burmans</a> move in; the chronicles date the founding of fortified Pagan to 849, but its oldest dated walls belong to 980 and its main walls to about 1020.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism.jpg" alt="Theravada Buddhism" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada Buddhism</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wat_Khung_Taphao_salakabhatta_2009.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">เทวประภาส มากคล้าย, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Pyu settlements were ruled by independent chiefs. At the larger cities the chiefs later styled themselves kings and set up courts modelled on Indian, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a>, concepts of monarchy, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/divine-kingship">divine kingship</a> was not fully adopted, because <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada Buddhism</a> stood in its way. Whether the great cities held the smaller towns as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> is unclear; by and large each <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-state</a> seems to have controlled only itself. The kings of Sri Ksetra titled themselves Varmans and Varma, and the city had at least two dynasties, perhaps three: the Vikrama dynasty, believed to have launched the Pyu calendar on 22 March 638, and a second founded by King Duttabaung on 25 March 739.</p><p>Indian culture was strongest in the south, where the sea trade came in: Sri Ksetra, "Field of Fortune", and Beikthano, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a>, bear Pali or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> names, and Sanskrit and Pali served as court languages beside Pyu, so that the 35 musicians of the embassy of 800–802 sang in Sanskrit before the Tang court. City plans joined indigenous circles with Indian squares and twelve gates, and Sri Ksetra's palace site of 518 by 343 metres symbolised both a mandala and a zata, a horoscope. Religion was Theravada Buddhism, probably from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andhra-region">Andhra region</a>, with Tantric and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahayana-buddhism">Mahayana Buddhism</a> and Hinduism widely present and pre-Buddhist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> sacrifice, drinking and a prominence of nuns persisting beside them.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/nagarjunakonda"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nagarjunakonda.jpg" alt="Nagarjunakonda" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/nagarjunakonda">Nagarjunakonda</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Buddhist_site_3rd_century_AD.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sabyk2001, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The thousand-year Pyu millennium linked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> to the classical states, and much of what Pagan became it owed to the Pyu. The Pyu script, based on Brahmi, may be the source of the Burmese script, and the Pyu were the first people in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a> to adapt Brahmic writing to a tonal language, inventing tonal markers. The calendar begun at Sri Ksetra in 638 became the Burmese calendar still in use in Myanmar. Halin's plan of twelve gates and cardinal entrances shaped Pagan and 19th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandalay">Mandalay</a>, Danyawaddy and Wethali in Arakan, and the Siamese city of Sukhothai a millennium later.</p><p>Pyu religious architecture was the prototype of Burma's. Its stupas and monasteries, in style, ground plan, brick size and technique, point to Amaravati and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagarjunakonda">Nagarjunakonda</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andhra-region">Andhra region</a>; by perhaps the 7th century the tall cylindrical stupas Bawbawgyi, Payagyi and Payama stood at Sri Ksetra. The Bebe and Lemyethna temples there were the models for Pagan's hollow gu temples, the solid stupas for the Shwezigon, Shwehsandaw and Mingalazedi and ultimately the Shwedagon in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangon">Yangon</a>, and the 13th-century Somingyi Monastery at Pagan repeats the plan of a 4th-century monastery at Beikthano. The Burman kings of Pagan claimed descent from the kings of Sri Ksetra and Tagaung back to 850 BCE, a claim most scholars dismiss, and the histories and legends of the Pyu became those of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamar-people">Burmans</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The millennium-old civilisation came down in the 9th century under repeated invasion from Nanzhao. Raids began in 754 or 760, Ko-lo-feng held the upper Irrawaddy by 763, and after the attacks of 800–802 and 808–809 the Nanzhao warriors overran the Pyu country in 832 and took 3,000 prisoners from Halin. The destruction was not total: the size of the realm and its many walled cities point to a population many times 3,000, no firm sign of violent overthrow has been found at any site, and people were still active at Halin about 870. More likely the raids so weakened the states that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamar-people">Burmans</a> could move into Pyu territory, gradually; modern scholarship holds that Burmans did not arrive in large numbers until the mid-to-late 9th century or even the 10th.</p><p>The Bamar set up a garrison town at Bagan at the confluence of the Irrawaddy and Chindwin; by the late 10th century they had taken over the leadership of the Pyu realm, and in the middle of the 11th century they founded the Pagan Empire, unifying the Irrawaddy valley and its periphery for the first time. Pyu settlements remained in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-burma">Upper Burma</a> for three centuries more, gradually absorbed into the expanding kingdom; Pyu artefacts occur across Pagan's settlements until about 1100. The Pyu language was still spoken in the late 12th century, but by the 13th the Pyu had assumed the Burman ethnicity and disappeared into history, their histories and legends incorporated into those of the Bamar.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Pyu <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-states</a> were the walled Tibeto-Burman cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-burma">Upper Burma</a>'s irrigated plains, from Beikthano to Sri Ksetra, which from about the 2nd century BCE traded between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada Buddhism</a>, Brahmic writing and the calendar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> still uses, and were weakened by Nanzhao's raids of 754–832 and absorbed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamar-people">Burmans</a> of Pagan by the mid-11th century.</em></p>

